"""Original introductory public-finance learning, without formal audit scoring."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(72, 'Public Finance Budget Analysis and Accountability', 'Public Finance\nBudget Analysis\nand Accountability',
        'Read a fictional public-service budget, distinguish allocation from delivery and propose a proportionate accountability enquiry without authorising expenditure or an audit.', [
        m('Read allocations execution and reliability', 'Separate budget promises, authorised allocations, spending and delivered services.', [
            'A public budget translates choices into planned resources over a defined period. Reading it requires knowing the unit, period, institutional coverage and status of each number. An announced allocation, an approved budget, a revised budget and an expenditure report describe different stages. The PEFA overview treats budget reliability and execution as important dimensions of public financial management. Here those dimensions prompt questions about an invented service budget; they are not a formal PEFA assessment, a legal opinion or an invitation to assign official scores. [S52]',
            'Begin an elementary comparison with like quantities. If an invented annual allocation is 120 units and recorded expenditure for the same year is 90, expenditure is three quarters of that allocation. The remaining 30 units are a difference requiring explanation, not automatic evidence of waste, theft or savings. Ask whether the scope and accounting basis match, whether commitments are included, whether a revision was authorised and whether entries are complete. A plausible explanation still needs a traceable record rather than confidence in the arithmetic alone. [S52]',
            'A financial entry does not establish that a public service reached its intended users. A transport allocation can be spent on an authorised contract while routes remain inaccessible to some residents. Pair the financial question with a bounded delivery question: what was purchased, what was supplied, who could use it and what evidence is available? Institutional accountability includes responsibility for following up recommendations, rather than merely circulating reports. Keep the learner role to reading supplied records and describing what a responsible reviewer should verify. [S54]',
            'Present the result as a short reconciliation note. Identify the two records compared, calculate the difference, distinguish observation from possible explanations and name the role that could resolve the uncertainty. State that invented numbers illustrate reasoning and do not represent any real authority. Do not call the difference a recovery, accuse a named person or promise a service improvement. If a record is absent, describe the consequence for confidence and request the appropriate non-personal record through a verified process. [S52]'],
            'An invented annual service budget lists 120 units approved and 90 spent. A publicity note calls the remaining 30 units stolen; neither revised estimates nor delivery records are supplied.',
            'Calculate the spending proportion, explain two alternative interpretations of the difference and write one delivery question with a responsible verification role.',
            'Ninety divided by 120 is 75 percent. The 30-unit difference requires reconciliation; it proves neither theft nor a completed service.', ['S52', 'S54'], [
                ('Budget stages describe different events.', 'Which pair must a learner distinguish in the invented budget?', 'An approved allocation and recorded expenditure.', ['An allocation and expenditure are always identical.', 'A publicity promise is the final ledger.', 'A revised estimate proves every service was delivered.'], 'Authorisation, recording and delivery are separate stages.'),
                ('Comparable quantities support transparent arithmetic.', 'Spending is 90 units against a comparable allocation of 120 units. What is the spending proportion?', '75 percent of the allocation.', ['30 percent of the allocation.', '120 percent of the allocation.', 'The figures alone prove criminal diversion.'], 'Divide 90 by 120 and multiply by 100; interpretation requires more evidence.'),
                ('An unexplained variance is a question rather than an accusation.', 'What is the strongest interpretation of the fictional 30-unit difference?', 'A variance requiring records and explanation.', ['A proven theft by a named official.', 'A guaranteed efficiency saving.', 'Proof that the allocation never existed.'], 'A difference can reflect several processes, including revisions or timing.'),
                ('Expenditure and service delivery need distinct evidence.', 'A contract payment is recorded but route accessibility is unknown. What follows?', 'Check supplied delivery evidence as a separate question.', ['The payment proves universal access.', 'The learner should authorise another payment.', 'A financial record is a complete service evaluation.'], 'Payment evidence does not settle who received a usable service.'),
                ('Introductory review does not confer assessment authority.', 'How should a learner describe use of the PEFA overview here?', 'A source of bounded questions without formal scoring authority.', ['An official PEFA certification.', 'A power to approve government accounts.', 'A substitute for all local financial rules.'], 'The course uses an overview, not an authorised assessment process.')]),
        m('Transparency access and meaningful scrutiny', 'Assess whether a fictional budget disclosure can support an accountable response.', [
            'Transparency starts with information that can be found, understood and connected to a decision. Uploading a document is an event; usable public scrutiny also depends on access, explanation and a route for questions. A Nigerian digital-budget case study proposes relationships between accessible information, institutions and value. Its abductive case approach develops propositions rather than measuring a national reduction in corruption. Use it to ask what conditions an invented disclosure needs, while keeping any claim about actual effects provisional and clearly separate from the classroom example. [R64]',
            'Check a disclosed record for its period, version, coverage, understandable units and connection to related records. An image-only table can be difficult to search or use with assistive technology. A file available only through a costly connection may exclude intended readers. Describing these barriers does not require collecting personal identities or testing a real government system. Work with the supplied fictional document and specify an accessible alternative that an authorised team could evaluate, such as a plain-language explanation alongside a readable data table. [S52]',
            'A scrutiny route needs an owner, an appropriate remit and a recorded next step. A public question may seek clarification, while an allegation requiring formal investigation belongs to a verified authority. Do not treat an unanswered email as proof of wrongdoing or invite learners to gather confidential complaint files. Accountability institutions operate within mandates, and follow-up matters after a recommendation is issued. Distinguish making a record public, receiving a question, examining it and communicating a reasoned response; each step needs its own evidence. [S54]',
            'Write an enquiry that can be answered from non-personal records: identify the fictional budget line, explain the inconsistency and ask for the relevant version or delivery explanation. Include a proposed acknowledgement and response route without inventing a statutory deadline. Record what remains uncertain, including whether the responsible institution has the staff or authority to act. This turns a broad promise of transparency into a testable process question while avoiding a claim that technology by itself guarantees honest administration or better public value. [R64]'],
            'A fictional portal publishes an unreadable budget image with no version date. Its contact inbox replies automatically, but nobody is assigned to explain conflicting figures.',
            'Identify three usability or responsibility gaps. Draft a non-accusatory enquiry and distinguish acknowledgement from a reasoned answer.',
            'A usable disclosure needs a clear record and a functioning response route. An automatic reply is evidence of receipt, not an explanation or corrective action.', ['R64', 'S52', 'S54'], [
                ('Disclosure and accountable response are separate.', 'An invented portal posts a file and sends an automatic receipt. What has not yet been demonstrated?', 'A reasoned response by a responsible role.', ['That every figure is correct.', 'That the recipient has received an audit qualification.', 'That corruption has necessarily ended.'], 'Publication and acknowledgement do not establish examination or correction.'),
                ('Contextual propositions are not measured causal effects.', 'How should the Nigerian digital-budget case study support this exercise?', 'As contextual questions about plausible mechanisms and conditions.', ['As a measured guarantee that every portal reduces corruption.', 'As a current budget law.', 'As a nationwide randomised effectiveness trial.'], 'An abductive case study develops propositions with contextual limits.'),
                ('Record versions matter for scrutiny.', 'Two invented tables disagree and neither has a date. What is the first bounded request?', 'Ask for the relevant dated versions and their scope.', ['Name a culprit immediately.', 'Average the figures and label them audited.', 'Assume the larger number is always current.'], 'Version and coverage differences must be resolved before interpretation.'),
                ('Accessibility concerns usable information.', 'What improves access to the fictional image-only table?', 'A readable table with an understandable explanation.', ['A larger publicity banner without the figures.', 'Requiring all users to disclose personal complaints.', 'Removing the version information.'], 'Readable content and explanation address an identified information barrier.'),
                ('Enquiries should match authority and evidence.', 'Which enquiry is proportionate for this learner exercise?', 'Ask a responsible role to explain a specified non-personal discrepancy.', ['Publish an unverified allegation about an individual.', 'Request confidential recipient files.', 'Promise a legally binding response date without checking rules.'], 'A precise records question remains within the supplied fictional scope.')]),
        m('Track correction and communicate contribution', 'Distinguish a recommendation, implemented action and evidenced service result.', [
            'A recommendation describes what an institution should consider or do; it is not proof that a correction occurred. The OECD public-audit summary distinguishes inputs, outputs, outcomes and impact, and asks institutions to recognise the difference between control and influence. A report may be under an institution\'s direct control while implementation depends on others. For this course, use that distinction to read a fictional follow-up record. Do not adopt a protected audit framework or suggest that an academy learner has become a statutory auditor. [S55]',
            'Build a simple chain of dated observations in your own words. A review note was issued; a responsible service manager accepted an action; a changed procedure was documented; a delivery record then showed a particular result. Each statement requires appropriate evidence. Acceptance alone does not prove implementation, and implementation does not settle whether the change caused a later result. Name alternative influences such as a revised allocation, staffing change or altered demand, and state which parts of the chain remain unverified rather than smoothing them into a success story. [S55]',
            'Equity is part of the follow-up question. An aggregate improvement can hide a persistent barrier for a group or location. Use invented, sufficiently broad service categories supplied in the case, and compare like periods with a clear denominator. Do not seek identifiable benefit records or infer motives from a small count. Institutional reporting can describe who is still missing from a service and recommend an appropriate check. It cannot turn an association into proof of intentional exclusion or authorise the learner to investigate private circumstances. [S54]',
            'A defensible public statement separates completion, observed change and attribution. For example, the fictional service adopted an accessible explanation and its supplied enquiries record shows fewer unresolved questions in a comparable period. That observation may justify continued monitoring, not a claim that the review eliminated all losses. State the source, limitations, responsible role and next review point. If the action is incomplete, say so clearly. Honest communication can show useful progress without treating a report count, a recommendation count or a media announcement as the final public outcome. [S55]'],
            'A fictional review issued six recommendations. Two have documented implementation, three were accepted without evidence of action, and one has no response. A publicity draft says all six improved services.',
            'Classify the six recommendations by evidence status. Rewrite the publicity claim and name one comparable delivery indicator and one alternative explanation.',
            'Only two actions have implementation evidence. Even those need separate outcome evidence and cautious attribution; acceptance and silence are different follow-up states.', ['S55', 'S54'], [
                ('Recommendations are outputs rather than demonstrated outcomes.', 'Six fictional recommendations were issued. What does that count establish?', 'The number issued, without proving implementation or service impact.', ['Six verified improvements.', 'Six legally recovered sums.', 'Six completed independent investigations.'], 'An issued recommendation is not the same event as a changed service.'),
                ('Acceptance differs from implementation.', 'Three managers accepted actions but supplied no implementation evidence. How should these be recorded?', 'Accepted, with implementation still unverified.', ['Implemented successfully.', 'Rejected because no publicity appeared.', 'Proven to have caused every later change.'], 'A commitment and an evidenced completed action require distinct statuses.'),
                ('Contribution differs from sole attribution.', 'A service improves after a correction and a simultaneous staffing increase. What is justified?', 'Describe the observed change and competing influences.', ['Attribute all change to the correction automatically.', 'Ignore the staffing change.', 'Claim no useful observation is possible.'], 'Concurrent changes limit a simple causal attribution.'),
                ('Aggregate results can conceal access differences.', 'Overall fictional response times improve while one access route remains unusable. What should follow-up include?', 'The unresolved route as well as the aggregate result.', ['Only the most favourable average.', 'Names and private histories of all users.', 'A conclusion that every group improved equally.'], 'An overall result does not establish equitable access across routes.'),
                ('Communication should match verified stages.', 'Which statement fits two evidenced actions and no verified outcomes?', 'Two actions are documented; service effects remain to be assessed.', ['All six recommendations transformed services.', 'All losses have ended.', 'The academy has certified a public audit.'], 'The statement identifies completed action without inventing impact.')])])
